## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `SerializerTests.cs`

**Summary: 4 Critical · 1 High · 1 Medium · 0 Low**

This suite has a systemic self-referential assertion problem. Five of eight tests use the input as the expected value for an operation that should transform it, meaning they pass tautologically and can never catch regressions.

---

### Critical Findings

**C1 · Self-referential assertion — `UpperCase_ToUpper_Works` (line 72–77)**

The input `"HELLO"` is already uppercase. `"HELLO".ToUpper()` is guaranteed to equal `"HELLO"` — the test never verifies that `ToUpper` actually transforms anything.

```csharp
// BEFORE (tautological — always passes)
var input = "HELLO";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual(input, result);

// AFTER (tests actual transformation)
var input = "hello";
var result = input.ToUpper();
Assert.AreEqual("HELLO", result);
```

---

**C2 · Self-referential assertion — `NormalizeName_AlreadyNormalized_NoChange` (line 37–42)**

The input `"alice"` is already lowercase and trimmed — exactly what `Normalize` produces. The assertion `input == Normalize(input)` is an identity check that can never fail.

```csharp
// BEFORE (input is already normalized — tautological)
var name = "alice";
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize(name);
Assert.AreEqual(name, result);

// AFTER (tests actual normalization behavior)
var result = NameNormalizer.Normalize("  ALICE  ");
Assert.AreEqual("alice", result);
```

---

**C3 · Self-referential assertion — `GetDisplayName_ReturnsFormattedName` (line 56–61)**

Production code: `GetDisplayName() => Name`. The test asserts `user.Name == user.GetDisplayName()`, which is asserting a field equals itself through a trivial accessor. The test name says "formatted" but nothing is formatted. This test can never fail unless the method is deleted.

```csharp
// BEFORE (asserts field == field, tautological)
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual(user.Name, display);

// AFTER (asserts against an independent expected value)
var user = new UserDto { Name = "Alice", Age = 30 };
var display = user.GetDisplayName();
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", display);
// If GetDisplayName is *supposed* to format (e.g. "Alice (30)"),
// fix the production code and assert the formatted string.
```

---

**C4 · Self-referential round-trip masks data loss — `MapToDto_MapBack_RoundTrip` (line 45–53)**

The mapper is **lossy**: `ToDto` drops `Id` and `Email`; `ToEntity` hard-codes `Id = 0` and `Email = ""`. A round-trip test on a lossy mapping gives false confidence — it implies the mapping preserves data when it doesn't. Worse, this test should actually *fail* (`entity.Id` is 1 but `backToEntity.Id` is 0), suggesting it may not be running, or the production code changed after the test was written.

```csharp
// BEFORE (round-trip on a lossy mapping — false confidence or outright failure)
var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
var backToEntity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Id, backToEntity.Id);        // 1 ≠ 0!
Assert.AreEqual(entity.FullName, backToEntity.FullName);
Assert.AreEqual(entity.Email, backToEntity.Email);   // "a@b.com" ≠ ""!

// AFTER (test each direction independently against known expected values)
[TestMethod]
public void MapToDto_MapsFieldsCorrectly()
{
    var entity = new UserEntity { Id = 1, FullName = "Alice Smith", Email = "a@b.com" };
    var dto = Mapper.ToDto(entity);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", dto.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, dto.Age); // document that Age is not mapped
}

[TestMethod]
public void MapToEntity_MapsFieldsCorrectly()
{
    var dto = new UserDto { Name = "Alice Smith", Age = 30 };
    var entity = Mapper.ToEntity(dto);
    Assert.AreEqual("Alice Smith", entity.FullName);
    Assert.AreEqual(0, entity.Id);    // document that Id is not mapped
    Assert.AreEqual("", entity.Email); // document that Email is not mapped
}
```

---

### High Finding

**H1 · Validate test only covers happy path — `Validate_ValidInput_ReturnsSameInput` (line 63–69)**

The test itself is technically correct — `ValidateEmail` does return the input when valid. But with no companion test for *invalid* input, the validation logic (`IsNullOrWhiteSpace`, missing `@`) is completely untested. This is the only method in the codebase that throws, yet no test verifies that behavior.

```csharp
// ADD (negative path tests)
[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_NullInput_Throws()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(() => Validator.ValidateEmail(null!));
}

[TestMethod]
public void ValidateEmail_MissingAtSign_Throws()
{
    Assert.ThrowsException<ArgumentException>(() => Validator.ValidateEmail("invalid"));
}
```

---

### Medium Finding

